## Remediation summary

### R-1 — behavioural reload-warning coverage

Notification construction moved to the pure `failureNotification` seam. Its
tests drive typed, unexpected, pinned and unpinned outcomes and assert the
actual returned operator message. A separate wiring assertion requires the
live `run` catch path to compute the post-failure pin state, pass it through
`notifyFailure`, and pass it into the pure builder. Removing the live argument
or the builder's behavior now fails.

### R-2 — bounded artifact-code diagnosis

`artifact_rejected` now shows a separately bounded `artifact_code`, such as
`unsupported_deployment`, while continuing to suppress all server-authored
prose. `SERVER_ARTIFACT_CODES` is held in exact cross-language equality with
the server's `ARTIFACT_REFUSAL_CODES`; an unknown or hostile value remains
hidden.

### R-3 — scratch deployment pin

The loopback-only `tools.snapshot_api` rehearsal launcher accepts four
rehearsal-namespaced environment variables as one all-or-nothing deployment
tuple. Partial, blank, overlong or unsafe values refuse startup. The default
remains `OBSERVED_DEPLOYMENT`, production composition does not read the
override, and startup prints the effective tuple for comparison with Foundry.
The operations guide documents the scope and disposable-database requirement.

### R-4 — duplicate-safe lost-pin reconciliation

The recovery query now selects the current attempt's append-only
`snapshot_submission.accepted` audit event by `audit_events.occurred_at`, then
joins `audit_events.entity_id` (the checksum) to the all-time
`foundry_snapshots` row. A duplicate therefore produces a current-window event
joined to the original snapshot even when that snapshot's `received_at` is old.
The result includes the audit payload's `duplicate` value.

The miss rule requires this audit-event query to be repeated and return no
accepted new or duplicate attempt before a fresh export can be authorized. The
guide explicitly prohibits restoring a `foundry_snapshots.received_at` filter.
Rehearsal A step 10 now requires the harder duplicate-response-loss case and an
expected hit with `duplicate = true`.

`tests/test_snapshot_recovery_documentation.py` requires the audit-to-snapshot
checksum join, the attempt-time audit bounds, the absence of snapshot-time
bounds, the duplicate output, and the duplicate-safe miss rule.

